body:
@mrjustaguy I discovered and fixed the bug that caused the instability, which is actually an upstream (#111512 ) problem. Now I fixed it upstream and rebased this PR. Could you please test again whether the stability improves and that Godot no longer crashes?  
If there isn't a stability problem present now, then we can inform the devs that this PR is functionally complete and ready for review. Your previous tests has already proved that FSR3 in Godot indeed has improvements over the current FSR2.   
As for the alpha problem, I will keep investigate it. I will open a new PR if I find ways to relieve it. As this problem is not solely applicable to FSR3, but also related to the existing FSR2 implementation, and it would require changes to the core pipeline (which is out of scope for this PR), and that such improvements would be unlikely to introduce API breaks, I would be prone to do that in a new PR.

>
> Still same issue

@jcostello I got it. I have to add macro defs to thirdparty code too. Now I fixed it, please try again.

@Calinou Sorry for my misconception. The problem that I proposed in my last comment is not the same as what you've encountered. My problem is reported as #111869 , whilst the one you met is caused by Mesa-NIR not supporting binding numbers larger than 1000.  
There are two ways of resolving this problem. A thorough solution is to change the way of how registers are calculated in the NIR part to make it support binding numbers larger than 1000. But this would be a intrusive change and would require approval from contributors of the DX12 backend. A more high-level and less intrusive workaround is to modify the binding IDs of those resources in FSR shaders, which is what I've done in the latest commit. Now all FSR upscalers works fine without errors on my local machine when running on DX12, please test again and see if the problem still persists.  
Anyway, if the first approach is more preferred, please tag me and I'll change the way I make it work with the Mesa-NIR driver.
